From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winlogon: a session's SYSTEM process, the stand-in remote-support services look for

On Windows every session has winlogon.exe, running as SYSTEM in that session.
UltraVNC's and AnyDesk's services find it by name and session, open its token
and start their per-session helper with a copy: SYSTEM's identity in the
person's session (with 9625/9626 such a helper gets its own display on the
person's screen, and capture and input through the compositor when it runs in
the services' control group). Wine had no winlogon.exe, so they fell back to
the person's own token (the lock screen black to them) or failed (i0634).

programs/winlogon is the stand-in: it does nothing but be there, and ends when
its standard input does. sg-session's sg-lockd starts it as SYSTEM in each
session it watches (SG_SESSION_UID) and holds that input. Its process and
token are SYSTEM's (sg_create_process_sd): SYSTEM may open them, and an
administrator's elevated program (SYSTEM here); a standard user, or an
administrator's ordinary program, may not -- as on Windows, where a standard
user cannot open winlogon for its token.

Gate: sg-session's test/winlogon-gate.sh (QA VM: a service finds the session's
winlogon.exe, duplicates its token and starts a program in that session as
SYSTEM; the person's program and a standard user cannot open it).
---
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -1801,6 +1801,7 @@
 enable_winepath
 enable_winetest
 enable_winhlp32
+enable_winlogon
 enable_winmgmt
 enable_winver
 enable_wmic
@@ -23313,6 +23314,7 @@
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winevdm enable_win16
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winhelp.exe16 enable_win16
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winhlp32 enable_winhlp32
+wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winlogon enable_winlogon
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winmgmt enable_winmgmt
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winoldap.mod16 enable_win16
 wine_fn_config_makefile programs/winver enable_winver
--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -3600,6 +3600,7 @@
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winevdm,enable_win16)
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winhelp.exe16,enable_win16)
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winhlp32)
+WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winlogon)
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winmgmt)
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winoldap.mod16,enable_win16)
 WINE_CONFIG_MAKEFILE(programs/winver)
--- /dev/null
+++ b/programs/winlogon/Makefile.in
@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@
+MODULE    = winlogon.exe
+
+EXTRADLLFLAGS = -mconsole -municode
+
+SOURCES = \
+	main.c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/programs/winlogon/main.c
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+/*
+ * winlogon.exe: a session's SYSTEM process (Stained Glass OS, 9657)
+ *
+ * On Windows every session has a winlogon.exe running as SYSTEM in that
+ * session. Remote-support services (UltraVNC, AnyDesk) find it by name and
+ * session, open its token, and start their per-session helper with a copy of
+ * it: SYSTEM's identity in the person's session. Stained Glass OS has no
+ * Winlogon -- signing in, locking and the secure screens are sg-session's --
+ * so this is the stand-in: sg-lockd starts it as SYSTEM in each session it
+ * watches (SG_SESSION_UID), and it does nothing but be there. Its process and
+ * token are SYSTEM's: only SYSTEM (and an elevated administrator, which is
+ * SYSTEM here) may open them, as on Windows, so a standard user cannot take
+ * its token. It ends when its standard input does: sg-lockd holds the other
+ * end, and closes it when the session goes (or sg-lockd does).
+ *
